@*
=====================================================================================
 Q3.2  CREATE VIEW & TempData DISPLAY   [15 marks]   Pointer 5: Razor Views, Pointer 6: Forms & Model Binding
=====================================================================================

 LESSON
 ------
 A Razor view is HTML with C# sprinkled in using the @ symbol.
   @model X           first line: "this page is strongly typed to X". Now Model.Name has IntelliSense.
   @if (...) { ... }  show something only when a condition is true
   @foreach (...)     repeat HTML for each item in a list
   at-star ... star-at   a Razor comment (like this very block)

 TAG HELPERS are the clever bit. They are attributes starting with asp- that turn into real HTML:
   <form asp-action="Create" method="post">    posts to the Create action (and adds the anti-forgery token)
   <label asp-for="Title">                      a label made from the property name
   <input asp-for="Title">                      an input with the right name, id, value and validation attributes
   <span asp-validation-for="Title">            shows the error message for that property
   <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly">     shows errors that belong to the whole model
   <select asp-for="AuthorId" asp-items="ViewBag.Authors">   a dropdown filled from a SelectList

 MODEL BINDING: when the form posts, MVC matches each input's name to a property of the same name
 and builds the object for you. asp-for writes those names, so they match automatically.

 WORKED EXAMPLE (a Book form)
 ----------------------------
   @model LibraryApp.Models.Book

   @{ ViewData["Title"] = "Add Book"; }

   @if (TempData["SuccessMessage"] != null)
   {
       <div class="alert alert-success">@TempData["SuccessMessage"]</div>
   }

   <form asp-action="Create" method="post">
       <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="text-danger"></div>

       <div class="mb-3">
           <label asp-for="Title"></label>
           <input asp-for="Title" class="form-control" />
           <span asp-validation-for="Title" class="text-danger"></span>
       </div>

       <div class="mb-3">
           <label asp-for="AuthorId">Author</label>
           <select asp-for="AuthorId" class="form-select" asp-items="ViewBag.Authors"></select>
       </div>

       <button type="submit" class="btn btn-success">Save</button>
   </form>

 COMMON TRAPS
 ------------
 * method="post" is missing -> the form sends a GET and your POST action never runs.
 * asp-for must name a PROPERTY of the @model class. A typo is a runtime error.
 * Closing </form> forgotten: the browser is forgiving, the exam marker is not.
 * TempData["x"] is compared with null before you show it. Without the @if you get an empty box.
 * The page needs @model Patient (singular). The Index page uses IEnumerable<Patient>. Don't mix them.
 * A Razor comment ends at the first star-at it meets, so never type that pair inside one.

 THE TASK
 --------
 Write the Razor view for the Patient Create page. It must contain:
   (a) the model declared as HospitalApp.Models.Patient
   (b) the page title (ViewData["Title"])
   (c) the TempData["SuccessMessage"] shown ONLY if it exists
   (d) the TempData["ErrorMessage"] shown ONLY if it exists
   (e) a form that posts to the Create action
   (f) a validation summary
   (g,h) label + input + validation span for FullName
   (i,j) label + input + validation span for Age
   (k) a dropdown (select) for DoctorId filled from the ViewBag
   (l) a submit button
   (m) the form closed
 The GET Create action that fills the ViewBag lives in PatientsController (Q3.1 task d).

 HINT
 ----
 The dropdown reads from  ViewBag.DoctorId  and binds with asp-for="DoctorId".
 The success box is a normal div; Bootstrap classes are alert alert-success / alert-danger.
 Tests only read this file as text, so you can check them without running the app. To see it live:
 cd HospitalApp, then dotnet run, then open /Patients/Create.

 >>>>> WRITE YOUR ANSWER BELOW THIS LINE <<<<<
*@
@model HospitalApp.Models.Patient

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Create Patient";
}

<h2>Create Patient</h2>

@if (TempData["SuccessMessage"] != null)
{
    <div class="alert alert-success">@TempData["SuccessMessage"]</div>
}

@if (TempData["ErrorMessage"] != null)
{
    <div class="alert alert-danger">@TempData["ErrorMessage"]</div>
}

<form asp-action="Create" method="post">

    <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="text-danger"></div>

    <div class="mb-3">
        <label asp-for="FullName" class="form-label"></label>
        <input asp-for="FullName" class="form-control" />
        <span asp-validation-for="FullName" class="text-danger"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="mb-3">
        <label asp-for="Age" class="form-label"></label>
        <input asp-for="Age" class="form-control" />
        <span asp-validation-for="Age" class="text-danger"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="mb-3">
        <label asp-for="DoctorId" class="form-label">Doctor</label>
        <select asp-for="DoctorId" class="form-select" asp-items="ViewBag.DoctorId"></select>
    </div>

    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-success">Create</button>
</form>

@section Scripts {
    @await Html.PartialAsync("_ValidationScriptsPartial")
}
